{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310637,"nonce":"24aXevzC09sNOEU6BUd3VBRs5DQ=","title":"IrFunctionId restarts at 0 in every module, so it cannot key anything that spans modules","message":"`IrModule::new` sets `next_function_id: 0` (compiler/src/ir/modules.rs:380) and\n`alloc_function_id` just increments it (:398-401). **Every module therefore mints\nIrFunctionId(0), (1), (2)... independently — the id is MODULE-LOCAL, not program-wide.**\n\nThe tiered backend nonetheless holds ONE program-wide\n`function_pointers: BTreeMap\u003cIrFunctionId, usize\u003e` (codegen/tiered_backend.rs:310) across\n`modules: Arc\u003cRwLock\u003cVec\u003cIrModule\u003e\u003e\u003e`. Multi-module is a real scenario here — 884dfaf9 exists\nprecisely because cross-module call targets needed NAMES to rebind by.\n\nCaught while keying OSR helper slots: keying by IrFunctionId would let two functions in\ndifferent modules share a slot, so a back edge would load a helper compiled for a DIFFERENT\nfunction's loop and jump into it with the wrong frame. Silent, and only under multi-module builds.\nSlots are keyed by function NAME instead (compiler/src/ir/osr.rs).\n\n**RULE (same one the TypeId punning incident produced): only FQNs/names cross contexts.**\nBefore keying ANY cross-module table by an id, check where its counter is initialised.\nSee [[bugs-stdtypes-type-addition-breaks-nue]], [[project-osr-beadie-helpers-unwired]].\n\n\n**The TieredBackend collision worry is DEAD.** `TieredBackend::modules` is always length 1: the\nfrontend merges every import and the stdlib into ONE IrModule before codegen (rebasing as it\ngoes — `compilation.rs:5225` import_base = 100_000 + N*10_000; `:7138` stdlib offset), `run_file`\ntakes `.last()`, and a .rzb is built as a single merged module (`preblade.rs:189`). Verified\nempirically: the postcard headers of all four .rzb on disk report `module_table = 1`.\nSo `function_pointers` and friends have one id space. Do not re-raise this.\n\n**Multi-file `rayzor aot` was predicted to miscompile. IT DOES NOT — measured.** The structural\nprecondition is real (`codegen/aot_compiler.rs:129` returns ALL modules; :240-245 declare-all then\ncompile-all-bodies into one backend whose `function_map` is id-keyed, llvm_jit_backend.rs:165).\nBut `rayzor aot App.hx Lib.hx --emit llvm-ir` emits `@aa/@bb/@cc` from the FIRST file and NOTHING\nfrom the second — Lib's functions are absent entirely. And a cross-file reference does not even\ntypecheck (\"Cannot find name 'Lib'\"). So multi-file AOT is NON-FUNCTIONAL, not dangerous: the CLI\naccepts `num_args = 0..` (src/main.rs:303) but extra files contribute no reachable code.\n\n**Load-bearing rule, from 884dfaf9:** an IrFunctionId is a SESSION-LOCAL SLOT NUMBER, not an\nidentity; the NAME is the identity across modules and across sessions. Three commits in this\nfamily: 4e9a1409 (monomorphizer ids collided with import renumbering, clobbering stdlib malloc =\u003e\nSIGSEGV), 8949c398 (imported globals aliased main-module statics), 884dfaf9.\n\n**Still unverified, worth a look someday:** `import_base = 100_000 + N*10_000` assumes no import\nmodule exceeds 10,000 functions and nothing checks it; and the merge `panic!` guard\n(`compilation.rs:7023`) only fires when NAMES DIFFER, so two files' `__vtable_init__` (both\nliterally that name, `qualified_name: None`) would be absorbed as a silent overwrite.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_irfunctionid_is_module_local` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}